reece) remembered for his religious works characterized by elongated human forms and dramatic use of color (1541-1614) [Domenikos Theotocopoulos, El Greco]</p><p><b>od ili koje se odnose na ili karakteristične za drevne Grke i Rimljane, posebno njihovu umjetnost, književnost ili kulturu (MT)</b></p><p><i>adj.</i> of or pertaining to or characteristic of the ancient Greeks and Romans, especially their art, literature, or culture [Graeco-Roman, classical]</p><p><b>arhitektura pod utjecajem drevnih Grka ili Rimljana (MT)</b></p><p><i>n.</i> architecture influenced by the ancient Greeks or Romans [classical architecture]</p><p><b>Bogotvo iz klasične mitologije. (MT)</b></p><p><i>n.</i> a deity of classical mythology [Graeco-Roman deity]</p><p><b>stil hrvanja u kojem hrvačima zabranjuje se da se hvataju ili trče ili koriste držale ispod struka (MT)</b></p><p><i>n.</i> a style of wrestling where the wrestlers are forbidden to tackle or trip or use holds below the waist</p><p><b>Grčka, Grčkoj</b></p><p><i>n.</i> a republic in southeastern Europe on the southern part of the Balkan peninsula [Ellas, Hellenic Republic]</p><p><i>n.</i> ancient Greece</p><p><b>pohlepa, požudnost</b></p><p><i>n.</i> excessive desire to acquire or possess more (especially more material wealth) than one needs or deserves</p><p><i>n.</i> reprehensible acquisitiveness [avarice, avaritia, covetousness, rapacity]</p><p><b>lakom, pohlepan, požudan, proždrljiv (od &quot;greedy&quot;)</b></p><p><i>adv.</i> in a greedy manner [avariciously, covetously]</p><p><b>pohlepa</b></p><p><i>n.</i> an excessive desire for food [hoggishness, piggishness]</p><p><i>n.</i> an excessive desire for wealth (usually in large amounts) [rapaciousness, voraciousness]</p><p><b>lakom, pohlepan, požudan, proždrljiv</b></p><p><i>adj.</i> immoderately desirous of acquiring e.g. wealth [avaricious, covetous, grabby, grasping, prehensile]</p><p><i>adj.</i> (often followed by `for') ardently or excessively desirous [avid, devouring, 