ijev zakon (MT)</b></p><p><i>n.</i> Jew who practices strict observance of Mosaic law</p><p><b>Židovi koji strogo poštuju Mojsijev zakon kako je tumačen u Talmudu, vjerovanja i običaje židovske sekte koja strogo slijedi Mojsijev zakon (MT)</b></p><p><i>n.</i> Jews who strictly observe the Mosaic law as interpreted in the Talmud [Jewish Orthodoxy]</p><p><i>n.</i> beliefs and practices of a Judaic sect that strictly observes Mosaic law</p><p><b>stanje ponavljajućeg sna u kojem se ne događaju brzi pokreti očiju i ne događa se sanjanje. (MT)</b></p><p><i>n.</i> a recurring sleep state during which rapid eye movements do not occur and dreaming does not occur [NREM, NREM sleep, nonrapid eye movement, nonrapid eye movement sleep]</p><p><b>ortodoksija, pravoslavlje</b></p><p><i>n.</i> the quality of being orthodox (especially in religion)</p><p><i>n.</i> a belief or orientation agreeing with conventional standards</p><p><b>praktičar ortopedije (posebno jedan od učenjaka iz 17. ili 18. stoljeća koji su predložili reformu engleskog pravopisa kako bi bolje odražavao izgovor) (MT)</b></p><p><i>n.</i> a practitioner of orthoepy (especially one of the 17th or 18th century scholars who proposed to reform English spelling so it would reflect pronunciation more closely)</p><p><b>ortoepija, izgovor</b></p><p><i>n.</i> the way a word or a language is customarily spoken [pronunciation]</p><p><i>n.</i> a term formerly used for the part of phonology that dealt with the `correct' pronunciation of words and its relation to `correct' orthography</p><p><b>ortogonalan, ortogonalni, pravokutan</b></p><p><i>adj.</i> not pertinent to the matter under consideration [extraneous, immaterial, impertinent]</p><p><i>adj.</i> statistically unrelated</p><p><i>adj.</i> having a set of mutually perpendicular axes [rectangular]</p><p><b>odnos protivljenja između stvari pod pravim kutom (MT)</b></p><p><i>n.</i> the relation of opposition between things at right angles [orthogonality, perpendicularity]</p><p